  - [1mTyped event enum[0m ([38;5;153mtask.assigned[39m, [38;5;153mtask.completed[39m, [38;5;153mcomment.added[39m, …). Each event type is a named thing with its own
    payload and its own notification copy. Buys precise rendering ("Alex assigned you [3mFix login[0m"), easy per-type
    preferences, and a cheap unread feed. Costs: adding a field that should notify means adding an event type and its
    copy — a small code change each time.
  - [1mGeneric field diffs[0m (record [38;5;153m{field, old, new}[39m for every mutation). Buys total coverage automatically and doubles as
    an audit log. Costs: notification copy becomes generic and worse ("[38;5;153mpriority[39m changed from [38;5;153m2[39m to [38;5;153m1[39m"), and you get noise
    from fields nobody cares about — every keystroke-level description edit is a notification unless you build
    suppression rules on top.
  - [1mHybrid[0m — a full diff/audit log underneath, with a mapping layer that promotes only [3minteresting[0m diffs into typed
    notification events. Buys both the audit trail and good copy. Costs: two concepts to maintain, and it's more than
    this app needs on day one.

  I recommend the [1mtyped event enum[0m. The diff approach looks more general but the generality lands in the wrong place:
  notifications are a human-readable product surface, and the per-type copy you'd have to write anyway is the actual
  work. The hybrid is the right end state [3mif[0m you later need an audit log — and going enum-first doesn't block it, since
  a diff log can be added underneath without changing the notification API.

  On the actual list, my starting set would be: assigned/unassigned, status changed, due date changed, comment added,
  and task deleted — deliberately [1mexcluding[0m title/description edits, which are the top source of notification fatigue in
  every tracker I know of.
